<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nodira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mohlaroyim</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nodira was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbeks">Uzbek</a> poet and the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umar-khan-of-kokand">Umar Khan of Kokand</a>. She wrote in Chagatai Turkic and Persian, and after her husband's death in 1822 she had great influence during the reign of her son Muhammad Ali Khan, supporting poets and founding schools. When Nasrullah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a> in 1842 she was executed together with her son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nodira.jpg" alt="Nodira" width="320" height="461" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Shamsroi Khasanova (1917–1956) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Painting_of_Heroine_Nodira_by_Shamsroi_Khasanova.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
